  {
    "@id": "http://purl.org/spar/bido-research-career-category",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/contributor": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
        "@value": "Sebastian Barzaghi (https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0799-1527)"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/creator": [
      {
        "@value": "Silvio Peroni (http://orcid.org/0000-0003-0530-4305)"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Francesco Osborne (https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6557-3131)"
      },
      {
        "@value": "Enrico Motta (https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0015-1952)"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/date": [
      {
        "@value": "2026-10-05"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\nThis is an additional module of BiDO that extends the class `bido:CategorialBibliometricData` of the core module with specific categories describing the research career of people. \n\n![The architecture of BiDO-Research-Career-Category module.](bido-research-career-category.png)\n\nThese categories are described by the class `bido:ResearchCareerCategory`, which is characterised by four specific dimensions that have been used by our algorithm to cluster citation data:\n* the _research period considered_, i.e., the interval of research years that the algorithm is taking into consideration (e.g., the first 5/10 years);\n* the _curve_, i.e., the specific shape proper to the clusters identified by the algorithm, which is characterised by a trend (flat/increasing/decreasing) and, in the latter two cases, by an acceleration or deceleration point (none or premature, median, overdue acceleration/deceleration);\n* the _slope_ of such curve, in terms of strength (low/moderate/high) and kind of growth (linear/polynomial/exponential/logarithmic);\n* the _order of magnitude_, which categorises the number of citations received in the considered period according to a uniform model of common-sense estimation [1], which describes intervals of half-order of magnitude – i.e., “[0,1)”, “[1,3)”, “[3,9)”, “[9,27)”, “[27,81)”, “[81,243)”, “[243,729)”, etc.\n"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/language": [
      {
        "@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"
      }
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title": [
      {
        "@value": "The BiDO Research Career Category module (BiDO-Research-Career-Category)"
      }
    ],
